   "title": "Jigawa Assembly approves constitutional amendment for state police",
   "url": "https://www.vanguardngr.com/2026/10/jigawa-assembly-approves-constitutional-amendment-for-state-police/",
   "domain": "vanguardngr.com",
   "source": "vanguardngr.com",
   "published": "Tue, 27 Oct 2026 17:19:55 +0000",
   "_dt": 1793121595.0,
   "ageDays": 2.68,
   "summary": "The Jigawa State House of Assembly has approved the proposed constitutional amendment seeking the establishment of state police in Nigeria. He explained that the proposed amendment was transmitted to the state Assembly for consideration after its passage by the Senate and the House of Representatives. The development comes after the National Assembly gave state Houses of Assembly a 30-day deadline to communicate their positions on the proposed constitutional amendment.",
   "summary_zh": "吉加瓦州议会已批准一项拟议的宪法修正案，旨在尼日利亚建立州警察。他解释道，该修正案在参议院和众议院通过后提交州议会审议。此前，国民议会要求各州议会在30天内就是否支持该拟议宪法修正案表明立场。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "The Jigawa State House of Assembly has approved the proposed constitutional amendment seeking the establishment of state police in Nigeria. The Speaker of the House, Haruna Aliyu Dangyatin, announced the approval during plenary, saying the move was aimed at strengthening community policing and improving security across the country. He explained that the proposed amendment was transmitted to the state Assembly for consideration after its passage by the Senate and the House of Representatives. According to him, the establishment of state police would help address security challenges at the grassroots by giving state governments greater responsibility for protecting lives and property. The approval is part of ongoing efforts to decentralise Nigeria’s policing system and strengthen the capacity of states to respond to security threats within their jurisdictions. The development comes after the National Assembly gave state Houses of Assembly a 30-day deadline to communicate their positions on the proposed constitutional amendment. State legislatures were directed to indicate whether they supported or opposed the bill as part of the constitutional amendment process. Comments expressed here do not reflect the opinions of Vanguard newspapers or any employee thereof",
